<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Zhaoxiang of Qin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">325–251 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as King Zhao of Qin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Zhaoxiang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> from 306 to 251 BCE, the longest reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. With the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-ju">Fan Ju</a> and the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a>, he won great victories over Han, Wei, Chu and Zhao, notably at Changping in 260 BCE. In 288 BCE he briefly took the title of Western Emperor, and in 256 BCE Qin ended the Zhou royal line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin.jpg" alt="King Zhaoxiang of Qin" width="320" height="302" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ChenDaoIsHere · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heshibi_%E5%AE%8C%E7%92%A7%E5%BD%92%E8%B5%B5%E6%AD%A6%E6%B0%8F%E7%A5%A0_%E9%87%91%E7%9F%B3%E7%B4%A23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
